WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.060
Could one fact about one tiny molecule explain three puzzles?

2
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:13.920
An insect stands on a pond, a paper towel drinks water upward, and salt vanishes

3
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:14.920
into a glass.

4
00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:21.503
Start with one water molecule: one oxygen atom and two hydrogen atoms.

5
00:00:22.823 --> 00:00:27.003
Each hydrogen shares electrons with the oxygen in a covalent bond.

6
00:00:28.263 --> 00:00:30.823
The molecule is bent, like a wide letter V.

7
00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:35.227
Oxygen pulls the shared electrons closer.

8
00:00:36.487 --> 00:00:39.927
So the oxygen end becomes slightly negative shown in red.

9
00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:44.687
Each hydrogen end becomes slightly positive shown in blue.

10
00:00:45.927 --> 00:00:47.887
A molecule with charged ends is polar.

11
00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:50.157
Bring in a neighbor.

12
00:00:51.477 --> 00:00:56.557
Opposite charges attract, so the blue end of one molecule is drawn to the red end of

13
00:00:56.557 --> 00:01:02.637
the next. That weak pull between molecules is a hydrogen bond, drawn as a dashed

14
00:01:02.637 --> 00:01:03.637
gold line.

15
00:01:04.417 --> 00:01:07.357
Liquid water holds countless jostling molecules.

16
00:01:08.897 --> 00:01:12.197
Gold lines snap on, break, and form again with new partners.

17
00:01:13.597 --> 00:01:18.097
The grey bonds inside each molecule stay put, because they are much stronger.

18
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.720
Here is the one idea.

19
00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.000
Water's charged ends tug on its neighbors, on ions, and on other polar molecules.

20
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:29.780
Now watch it do three jobs.

21
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:32.530
Job one is dissolving.

22
00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:38.630
Salt is made of charged particles called ions, positive sodium and negative

23
00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:44.830
chloride. Water's red ends face each sodium, and its blue ends face each chloride.

24
00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:49.350
That pull of opposite charges lifts each ion off the crystal.

25
00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:51.850
So water is a solvent.

26
00:01:52.983 --> 00:01:59.823
Now add some oil. Oil molecules are mostly nonpolar, with few charged spots to grab.

27
00:02:01.143 --> 00:02:05.663
Water molecules tug on each other more than on oil, so the oil stays in its own

28
00:02:05.663 --> 00:02:06.663
drop.

29
00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:09.933
Job two is sticking to itself, called cohesion.

30
00:02:11.373 --> 00:02:14.893
At the surface, neighbors pull each molecule sideways and down.

31
00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:18.053
So the surface resists being stretched.

32
00:02:19.333 --> 00:02:23.113
That is surface tension, and it is still liquid water, not a skin.

33
00:02:24.217 --> 00:02:26.537
Job three is sticking to other things.

34
00:02:27.817 --> 00:02:31.457
Paper is made of cellulose fibers, with many polar parts.

35
00:02:32.797 --> 00:02:34.977
Water clings to them, and that is adhesion.

36
00:02:36.337 --> 00:02:39.537
Each climbing molecule pulls its neighbors up by cohesion.

37
00:02:40.897 --> 00:02:42.697
Together they give capillary action.

38
00:02:43.767 --> 00:02:44.987
Back to the strider.

39
00:02:46.307 --> 00:02:49.007
Its legs are covered in tiny water-repellent hairs.

40
00:02:50.407 --> 00:02:52.867
Will water climb those hairs like it climbs the towel?

41
00:02:54.227 --> 00:02:55.227
Pause and decide.

42
00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:08.220
No. Water clings only weakly to water-repellent hairs, so it pulls itself into

43
00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:13.560
beads. Surface tension, plus those hairs, lets a small strider stand.

44
00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:19.610
So one molecule's bent shape and charged ends help explain all three jobs.

45
00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:24.550
The same tug helps make water slow to warm, as film two shows.
